---

## Breaking it down



#### **1. The data (array of objects)** 

<img src="https://images.code.org/0b65b2bb3aa1f996960173280e8430cb-image-1767807082322.png" alt="array of students with scores in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">

#### This is the data the app starts with. 
#### Each student is an object, and all the students together are stored in an array. 

<br><br>


#### **2. The sort logic (inside a function)**

<div style="clear:both;"></div>
<img src="https://images.code.org/82ab47eabbc680e18ae36151e17c01b2-image-1767807127427.png" alt="function with sorting algorithm" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">


#### This function defines the sort rule.
#### It compares two students at a time and decides which one should come first based on score.

> #### `sort()` is a JavaScript array function that reorders items in a list.
> #### When you provide a **compare function**, you control what “first” means (highest score, A–Z, newest, etc.).

<br><br>


#### **3. Using the function**

<img src="https://images.code.org/f6ec4d1caba87b0013b42acc2abe3e42-image-1767807197683.png" alt="calling the sorting function in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">

#### This line runs the function using the original data.
#### The result is the same list of students, but reordered so the highest scores appear first.

